const DEEP_X={};
function addDeep(o){Object.assign(DEEP_X,o);}
addDeep({
"2026-10-29":{c:[["Eph 6:10-20","The last paragraph of Ephesians before the greetings. Chapters 1–3 set out God's plan to unite all things in Christ; chapters 4–6 draw out the way of life that follows (unity, new life, households). Paul now steps back and names the real enemy behind all the struggles he has described, then asks for prayer for himself, writing as 'an ambassador in a chain', that is, a prisoner, probably in Rome."],["Luke 13:31-35","Luke's “travel narrative” (9:51–19:27): Jesus has 'steadfastly set his face to go to Jerusalem' (9:51). He is in Herod Antipas's territory (Galilee or Perea). Some Pharisees, perhaps friendly, perhaps sent to scare him off, warn him of Herod, the man who beheaded John the Baptist."]],
 m:[["Eph 6:12","Paul is not saying human opponents do not matter. He says that behind persecution, temptation and division stand created spiritual persons, fallen angels, who are real but finite. So the Christian's weapons are of the same order: truth, justice, faith, the word of God and prayer."],["Luke 13:32-33","'To-day and to-morrow, and the third day' is a Semitic idiom for a short, fixed time under God's control. 'I am consummated' (Greek teleioumai, “I reach my goal”) points to his death and resurrection in Jerusalem. Jesus' timetable is set by the Father, not by Herod."],["Luke 13:34-35","The lament is Jesus speaking as the Lord of Israel, with the image used of God himself in the Old Testament (Deut 32:11; Ps 90:4, 'he will overshadow thee with his shoulders'). 'Behold your house shall be left to you desolate' foretells the Temple's destruction in AD 70. 'Blessed is he that cometh' anticipates Palm Sunday (19:38) and his final coming."]],
 s:{f:"Luke 13:34 (the hen and her brood)",
  l:"Jesus grieves over Jerusalem, which has rejected the prophets and will reject him, and foretells the city's ruin. He uses a homely image of a hen sheltering her chicks to describe his repeated wish to gather Israel.",
  a:"Jerusalem stands for the people of God who can refuse their Lord; the hen is Christ, who on the Cross stretches out his arms to gather the scattered children of God into one (John 11:52). Augustine notes 'how a hen becomes weak with her chickens' and sees in it Christ, who made himself weak to nourish the weak (Tract. in Ioh. 15.7).",
  m:"'Thou wouldest not': grace is offered but can be refused. Each soul is a little Jerusalem that can harden itself by delay. The moral lesson is to let ourselves be gathered now, in confession and prayer, rather than 'to-morrow'.",
  n:"'You shall not see me till … you shall say: Blessed is he that cometh' points beyond Palm Sunday to the Second Coming and to the heavenly Jerusalem, where those who were gathered under his wings will see him face to face (Rev 21:2–3)."}}
});
